            <ArticleTitle>How Rare Is an Oral Presentation of Myeloid Sarcoma in the Infant?</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0278-2391(17)31157-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.joms.2017.08.034</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Myeloid sarcomas of the oral cavity are exceedingly rare. This report describes a recent case, and reviews the literature. This case report serves three purposes: 1) to demonstrate that the use of an intraoral biopsy can diagnose severe systemic disease; 2) to remind practitioners to be cognizant of less common diagnoses in the differential diagnosis of facial swelling; and 3) to contribute a case of myeloid sarcoma that was confirmed by flow cytometry to the published data.</AbstractText>
